ID: B3-Ch10c-Collector
TEXT:

 "You won't ever forget it?" Eiden's smile widens by a fraction, the curve of his mouth effortless and amused. "Not even if I offer you a rare find from my travels?"

He draws a smooth, dark mineral from his coat pocket and extends it on an open palm. Melindra keeps her back half-turned, her chin lifted in a fierce attempt at defiance after the silence hex in Thilias, but her eyes drop to his hand and stay there. The stone catches the weak forest light with an oily, obsidian sheen.

"Even for a collector of your taste, pieces like this do not surface often," Eiden says, rolling the mineral across the backs of his knuckles without looking at it. "What was your name for them again? Deitrah... Dengah..."

"A Dedra stone," Flower and I blurt in unison.

"Ah, a Dedra stone!" Eiden's eyebrows arch, his closed eyelids crinkling in genuine delight.

His head tilts, drifting from Flower to me, then slides past my shoulder toward Kate. She stands five paces off, caught entirely unbraced—ruts of white frost crusting over her knuckles, her shoulders locked rigid, chest barely rising. The stillness holding her is a physical weight, the silent horror of being pinned helpless while an immortal chats across the clearing.

"Congratulations, Melindra," Eiden says softly. "It seems you found decent company while I was away."

"I'll take the stone," Melindra snaps. She darts forward, snatching the dark mineral from his palm before his fingers can close around it. A flush burns high on her ears. "Consider it an apology. But do not think you are forgiven."

"Of course," Eiden says, his tone smooth as polished glass.

 "Are you going to explain why you are here," Illuna says, her voice cutting cold and level from Flower's mouth as her eyes burn brilliant sapphire, "or should we start guessing?"

"All in good time, Illuna of the sacred woods massacre," Eiden replies without turning his head. "First, an unrelated curiosity. Was there not an armored white mage traveling in your company? Whatever became of Daren? Tall, dark-skinned, encased in steel plate from sunrise to sundown?"

"I am standing right in front of you," Daren says, his voice flat. "Stop pretending you cannot see me."

Daren is standing in a plain linen tunic, sleeves rolled to the elbows, his heavy plate armor stowed deep inside Hadrik's expandable leather ale-backpack. Without three stones of tempered steel between him and a stillwater, his broad shoulders look unnervingly bare against the pines.

"My good sir, you must be mistaken," Eiden says, turning toward him with exaggerated courtesy. "Beyond the conspicuous absence of plate armor, the Daren I know would have drawn steel by now, or at the very least glared at me as though measuring the depth of my throat. I recall no pleasant conversations between us since our last meeting, so what has changed?"

"Right. You wouldn't remember," Daren murmurs, crossing his arms over his chest. "You have no way of remembering."

"Pardon?"

"Let us say a different version of you, in another world, offered me a hand when I had hit rock bottom," Daren says, his jaw tightening on the memory. "Because of that, I am inclined toward patience. For now."

Eiden's polite smile vanishes. The air in the clearing drops a fraction of a degree.

"...You speak of the God of Time's trials."

"Yes," Daren says.

 "I see." Eiden stands entirely motionless, the humor wiped clean from his pale features. "In that case, take this as fair warning. That Eiden and I share neither memory nor mind. Depending on how far back the ritual reached, that version of me may have held a view of this world entirely alien to mine. Whatever he promised or confessed inside that trial does not apply here. Build your hopes on it and you will only set yourself up for disappointment."

"Do not worry," Daren says, holding his ground in bare linen. "I have no intention of confusing the two."

"Good," Eiden says, the easy, pleasant smile sliding back over his face like a mask. "With that settled, let us turn to the first matter that brought me here. Ollendor. Tell me, Barry, what do you think about their new king?"

choice(""I think he will be a great leader."", B3-Ch10c-Leader, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Leader, v_b3_ch10_leader = 1)
choice(""I don't really care that much about him, to be honest."", B3-Ch10c-Honest, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Honest, v_b3_ch10_leader = 0)
