ID: B3-Ch10c-Leader
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch10_behind,1) if (v_b3_ch4_cut == 0 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0)
achievement("No man left behind",v_ac_b3_ch10_behind)
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The underground king's right hand?" Eiden arches a pale brow, his head tilting slightly. "That is your verdict?"

"He beats the predecessor by a mile," I say, meeting his closed gaze.

"A low bar to clear," Eiden remarks. "I am asking if you truly believe he was the best choice on the board."

"Why not?" I say. "Albert ran the rebel courier network long before he ever served in the underground palace. He knows how to manage logistical friction and hold an organization together under pressure."

"I see," Eiden says softly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 1 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The underground king's right hand?" Eiden arches a pale brow, his head tilting slightly. "That is your verdict?"

"He beats the predecessor by a mile," I say, meeting his closed gaze.

"A low bar to clear," Eiden remarks. "I am asking if you truly believe he was the best choice on the board."

"Why not?" I say. "Peter was the most popular figure among the rebels, and easily the most decent man in the caverns. He has the temperament to heal fractures and rebuild the kingdom."

"I see," Eiden says softly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 2 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 || v_b3_ch8_king == 3 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 || v_b3_ch8_king == 4 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The king of the underground?" Eiden arches a pale brow, his head tilting slightly. "That is your verdict?"

"He beats the predecessor by a mile," I say, meeting his closed gaze.

"A low bar to clear," Eiden remarks. "I am asking if you truly believe he was the best choice on the board."

"Why not?" I say. "The man has spent three decades running a shadow city beneath Ollendor's streets. He understands the mechanics of leverage and power."

"I see," Eiden says softly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"The Overseer?" Eiden taps his chin with a pale index finger. "Last I checked, he was drafting fresh mage captives into the seredium towers to expand drone output."

"That bastard," I mutter, my jaw clenching. "He swore the tower issue would be settled."

"And it was," Eiden says, his smile widening faintly. "From the Overseer's perspective, the sole issue was operating below peak capacity. He resolved it, precisely as promised."

"He will regret it," I say. "The moment we have the strength to tear down his core, we return to Ollendor and leave his scrap in the same ditch as the last king."

"I see," Eiden says softly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"Breynan or the Overseer?" Eiden asks.

"Both of them," I say. "They balance each other out. Breynan brings human conscience; the machine brings logistical order. As long as they maintain that friction, the city stands a chance."

"Fascinating," Eiden murmurs, his hands resting loose in his pockets. "Word reaches me that the Overseer is re-engineering the towers to run without shackled mages. A peculiar waste of computing cycles for a machine with no moral compass. Did your group negotiate that?"

"It was the non-negotiable condition for our help," I say. "He agreed to unhook the prisoners the moment he devises an alternate planar feed."

"Interesting," Eiden says softly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 "I think he will be a great leader."

"A goblin healer on the throne is certainly an inventive choice," Eiden says. "I was equally intrigued by the underground king's sudden cooperative streak during the transition."

"What do you mean?" I ask, stepping forward. "What happened?"

"You have not heard?" Eiden asks, amused. "Your goblin companion insisted on an immediate release of every captive in the seredium towers. Had he severed them instantly, the city's entire infrastructure would have collapsed—transports falling from the sky, wards failing, total panic. To prevent riots, the underground king seeded whispers across the districts for two days, exposing the torture cells and the previous monarch's atrocities. By the time the towers powered down, crowds were marching in the streets demanding the mages' release. Your goblin simply granted the public will."

"Clever," I say. "If the citizens demanded the shutdown themselves, they could hardly revolt when the platforms stopped hovering."

"There were still grievances, but far fewer than a sudden blackout would have sparked," Eiden says. "The odds of civil war dropped to near zero. A remarkable outcome, given the volatility of the region."

"Good," I say.

Silence settles over the clearing for several seconds, broken only by the crackle of pine needles in the dying embers.

"Is that all you came to ask?"

"On that subject, yes," Eiden says.

"And the second matter?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing as she steps up beside me.

"A timely question, Kate of the Beacon of Hope," Eiden says. "This concerns you above all others. Waiting for your little band to reach your next milestone grew tedious, so I came to quicken your pace. Your upcoming encounter interests me as much as it does you."

"Why would our destination interest you?" Kate asks, her guard up.

"...You do not know where you are going?" Eiden's eyes remain shut, but his head tilts in genuine surprise.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king != 5 || v_b3_ch8_overseer2 != 0) {
 Silence settles over the clearing for several seconds, broken only by the crackle of pine needles in the dying embers.

"Is that all you came to ask?"

"On that subject, yes," Eiden says.

"And the second matter?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing as she steps up beside me.

"A timely question, Kate of the Beacon of Hope," Eiden says. "This concerns you above all others. Waiting for your little band to reach your next milestone grew tedious, so I came to quicken your pace. Your upcoming encounter interests me as much as it does you."

"Why would our destination interest you?" Kate asks, her guard up.

"...You do not know where you are going?" Eiden's eyes remain shut, but his head tilts in genuine surprise.

He turns toward Melindra. Her posture stiffens instantly.

}
 "Melindra, you have not told them?" Eiden asks.

"I was going to!" Melindra says, her voice jumping an octave as her fingers twist into her sleeves. "I was waiting for the right moment!"

"There is no time like the present," Eiden says smoothly.

"Melindra, what is this?" Kate steps forward, her dark eyes narrowing. "Who are you taking us to? What does this have to do with me?"

"Kate, listen to me," Melindra says, hands fluttering up in front of her chest. "First, stay calm. Promise me you will stay calm."

"Fine," Kate says, her jaw tight. "I promise."

"Right," Melindra takes a ragged, shallow breath. "When we struck our deal in Ollendor, I knew nothing about artificial mages or the Beacon of Hope. I only learned about all that later, listening to you talk around the campfires."

"Understood," Kate says. "Why bring it up now?"

"Because..." Melindra swallows hard, looking between Kate and the ground. "The contact with the pinecone detector... my acquaintance... she is--"

"Meridith," Eiden cuts in, sparing her the agony. "She is taking you to Meridith."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Message, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Message)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Honest
TEXT:

 "I don't really care that much about him, to be honest."

"You don't?" Eiden's brow arches, a faint smirk touching his lips. "Curious. I was under the impression your company held considerable sway over Ollendor's succession."

"We pushed a few pieces around the board, but it hardly matters in the end," I say. "The old tyrant is dead. Whoever sits on that throne is almost guaranteed to be an improvement."

"I see," Eiden says softly.

Silence settles over the clearing for several seconds, broken only by the crackle of pine needles in the dying embers.

"Is that all you came to ask?"

"On that subject, yes," Eiden says.

"And the second matter?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing as she steps up beside me.

"A timely question, Kate of the Beacon of Hope," Eiden says. "This concerns you above all others. Waiting for your little band to reach your next milestone grew tedious, so I came to quicken your pace. Your upcoming encounter interests me as much as it does you."

"Why would our destination interest you?" Kate asks, her guard up.

"...You do not know where you are going?" Eiden's eyes remain shut, but his head tilts in genuine surprise.

He turns toward Melindra. Her posture stiffens instantly.

 "Melindra, you have not told them?" Eiden asks.

"I was going to!" Melindra says, her voice jumping an octave as her fingers twist into her sleeves. "I was waiting for the right moment!"

"There is no time like the present," Eiden says smoothly.

"Melindra, what is this?" Kate steps forward, her dark eyes narrowing. "Who are you taking us to? What does this have to do with me?"

"Kate, listen to me," Melindra says, hands fluttering up in front of her chest. "First, stay calm. Promise me you will stay calm."

"Fine," Kate says, her jaw tight. "I promise."

"Right," Melindra takes a ragged, shallow breath. "When we struck our deal in Ollendor, I knew nothing about artificial mages or the Beacon of Hope. I only learned about all that later, listening to you talk around the campfires."

"Understood," Kate says. "Why bring it up now?"

"Because..." Melindra swallows hard, looking between Kate and the ground. "The contact with the pinecone detector... my acquaintance... she is--"

"Meridith," Eiden cuts in, sparing her the agony. "She is taking you to Meridith."

