ID: B3-Ch10c-Available
TEXT:

 "I think he was the least bad choice available at the time."

"Oh?" Eiden's brow arches, his head tilting with quiet amusement. "I was under the impression your company held considerable sway over Ollendor's succession. Am I to understand your backing rested solely on him being the least dreadful option?"

"Pretty much," I say with a shrug. "Given the state of the city, surviving without a bloodbath was the only metric that mattered."

"I see," Eiden says softly.

Silence settles over the clearing for several seconds, broken only by the crackle of pine needles in the dying embers.

"Is that all you came to ask?"

"On that subject, yes," Eiden says.

"And the second matter?" Kate asks, her brow furrowing as she steps up beside me.

"A timely question, Kate of the Beacon of Hope," Eiden says. "This concerns you above all others. Waiting for your little band to reach your next milestone grew tedious, so I came to quicken your pace. Your upcoming encounter interests me as much as it does you."

"Why would our destination interest you?" Kate asks, her guard up.

"...You do not know where you are going?" Eiden's eyes remain shut, but his head tilts in genuine surprise.

He turns toward Melindra. Her posture stiffens instantly.

 "Melindra, you have not told them?" Eiden asks.

"I was going to!" Melindra says, her voice jumping an octave as her fingers twist into her sleeves. "I was waiting for the right moment!"

"There is no time like the present," Eiden says smoothly.

"Melindra, what is this?" Kate steps forward, her dark eyes narrowing. "Who are you taking us to? What does this have to do with me?"

"Kate, listen to me," Melindra says, hands fluttering up in front of her chest. "First, stay calm. Promise me you will stay calm."

"Fine," Kate says, her jaw tight. "I promise."

"Right," Melindra takes a ragged, shallow breath. "When we struck our deal in Ollendor, I knew nothing about artificial mages or the Beacon of Hope. I only learned about all that later, listening to you talk around the campfires."

"Understood," Kate says. "Why bring it up now?"

"Because..." Melindra swallows hard, looking between Kate and the ground. "The contact with the pinecone detector... my acquaintance... she is--"

"Meridith," Eiden cuts in, sparing her the agony. "She is taking you to Meridith."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Message, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Message)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Message
TEXT:

 "Meridith as in... the leader of the lessathi?" Kate's voice drops into a register colder than her frost.

"I am so sorry!" Melindra's words spill out in a desperate rush, her hands pressing flat against her collarbones. "I swear I knew nothing about your past with her when we made our deal in Ollendor! When I figured it out, I tried to handle it alone and fetch the pinecone detector without dragging you into it, but she wouldn't budge. She grilled me on the transceiver about why I needed the locator and who I was traveling with. The moment I mentioned Daren, she connected the dots, figured out you were with us, and demanded to see you. She said unless you ask for the device in person, I get nothing. Kate, I know how terrible this sounds, but give me time! I can fix this!"

Behind her, Daren, Hadrik, Flower, and Illuna exchange sharp, tense whispers. From the golden amulet resting against Flower's chest, Arraka's laughter rings out, shrill, ancient, and thoroughly delighted. I catch Eiden's smirk turned squarely in my direction, his eyelids shut as ever.

"Aren't you pleased, Barry?" Eiden murmurs, his voice carrying effortlessly over the muttering. "Now you finally have the opportunity to deliver my message."

Eiden's message. With the chaos in Ollendor, it had slipped my mind entirely. He still expects me to hand it over.

 "Melindra, look at me," Kate says, closing the distance and taking Melindra firmly by both shoulders. "Take a deep breath and let your shoulders drop. Do it now."

"Right. Deep breath. Doing it," Melindra pants, her chest heaving in exaggerated gulps of air that do nothing to steady her racing pulse. "What now?"

"Listen carefully," Kate says, her grip steady. "Meeting Meridith face to face is fine. Set up the rendezvous."

"It's... fine?" Melindra blinks, disoriented, staring into Kate's eyes. "You mean it?"

"A week ago I might have refused," Kate says. "During the God of Time's first trial, Meridith kept me alive. Without her intervention, I would not have survived the chamber. I owe her the benefit of the doubt, especially when Diane told me she chose to work alongside her freely."

"So I can contact her?" Melindra asks, her voice trembling with relief. "Tell her we will meet whenever she is ready?"

"Yes," Kate says.

"The relief..." Melindra lets out a long, shuddering breath. "I thought you were going to draw a blade on me. Pin me to a trunk with a knife at my throat."

 "I would never do that to a friend," Kate says evenly.

Her dark eyes flick to me, a pointed look that makes it painfully clear she knows exactly who told Melindra about the dagger incident in the forest all those months ago.

"Isn't that right, Barry?"

"Naturally," I say quickly, clearing my throat. "Melindra was imagining the worst. Nothing to worry about."

"Then I am going to set up the channel right now," Melindra says, backing up two paces.

"Go," Kate tells her.

"Back in a moment," Melindra calls out as an updraft catches her robes, lifting her straight into the pine canopy.

"That concludes my business with your little circle," Eiden says, stepping backward as the wind settles. "We will cross paths again soon enough."

"You know where we are," Daren says, standing tall in his linen tunic.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Revenant, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Condition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Condition) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant
TEXT:

 "One last curiosity before I take my leave," Eiden says, pausing mid-step, his boot hovering an inch above the pine needles. "Do any of you know why your invisible revenant friend has been hovering behind me with a dagger drawn and her aura clamped shut for the last ten minutes?"

"Damn it, I hoped he would miss her," Arraka grumbles from the golden amulet as the air warps, light bending like heat shimmer until the revenant materializes in the clearing.

"Rose..." Kate's voice catches, tightening into something sharp and brittle at the sight of the herbalist's face worn by a walking corpse.

The revenant stands motionless, her skin grey and bloodless, her eyes clouded with the sickly yellow film of the God of Death. She holds a notched steel dagger leveled at Eiden's back.

"Can I assist you with something?" Eiden asks her, tilting his head over his shoulder without bothering to turn his body.

"My intent was to freeze time and strike at your most vulnerable moment, as you initiated your teleportation," the revenant states, her voice a flat, hollow drone with none of Rose's warmth. "That window is closed."

"A servant of the God of Death, if I am not mistaken," Eiden says mildly. "Why would your deity seek my demise?"

 "My master learned through a reliable source that you orchestrated the disruption on the magical plane earlier today," the revenant replies. "He demands retribution for your arrogance."

The disruption earlier today. Jirinn must have already set the gods at each other's throats across the planar divide, exactly as Arraka outlined.

"I have no notion of what you speak of," Eiden says, faintly puzzled. "I have not meddled in the affairs of the magical plane for centuries."

"My mandate is execution, not interrogation," she says.

"By all means," Eiden smirks, turning his head back toward us. "Give it your best effort."

She vanishes. In the exact same heartbeat she snaps into reality beside Eiden's shoulder, steel flashing in a lethal arc toward his carotid—and ricochets violently backward through the air as an invisible barrier erupts, slamming her hard into the pine needles without Eiden lifting a finger.

"Did you assume I wander about without automated countermeasures against temporal manipulation?" Eiden asks, looking down at her. "The God of Death lacks ambition if he dispatches a lone knife to take my head."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Revenant2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant2)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant2
TEXT:

 "The God of Death... does not... trust me..." The revenant pushes herself up from the damp soil, her breath rasping through dead lungs after the impact. "I am merely... a disposable tool. A test... of allegiance."

"How pathetic," Eiden remarks, hands resting easy in his coat.

"I am not... finished." She flickers and dissolves into the ether, reappearing behind Flower in the same breath, a cold blade pressed tight against the girl's small neck.

"Rose?" Flower tilts her head back, looking up into those vacant, yellowed eyes without fear.

"I apologize," the revenant says, the words mechanical and dead. "The God of Death commanded me to secure you as a hostage if the assassination failed."

"It is fine," Flower says softly, her voice small and tender. "I do not blame you."

 "Release her," Daren commands, his greatsword clearing its scabbard in a single, ringing arc that shears the air.

"The captive remains with me until the stillwater complies," she states, the knife edge pressing against Flower's collar. "Not a second before."

"And why should her survival concern me?" Eiden asks, turning slowly with his eyes shut.

"My master knows the golden fox requested your protection on her behalf," the revenant says. "Yield, or she dies."

"Your intelligence is flawed," Eiden replies, the smile on his lips turning icy. "Eleya asked that I refrain from killing her myself. She said nothing of playing guardian."

"She dies if you refuse!" the revenant presses.

 "Go right ahead," Eiden says, his smile widening into something genuinely monstrous. "You would do me a service. She remains the sole vessel capable of housing Arraka if that amulet ever shatters."

A suffocating silence drops over the clearing. The revenant stares at him, searching for a tremor, a tell, any sign of bluff in his relaxed posture. There is none.

"Proceed," Eiden urges quietly. "What stays your hand?"

The blade bites a fraction deeper into Flower's skin, drawing a thin red bead, but the revenant's fingers suddenly twitch. Her knuckles tremble against the hilt. Despite claiming to be nothing more than Death's hollow instrument, some buried instinct, some remnant of the woman who gathered herbs and healed wounds, is fighting against the command.

There is an opening to talk her down.

choice("Try to reason with the revenant.", B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_b3_ch10_reason = 1)
choice("Threaten the revenant.", B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant3, v_b3_ch10_reason = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant3
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch10_reasonable,1) if (v_b3_ch10_reason == 1)
achievement("Let's be reasonable!",v_ac_b3_ch10_reasonable)
#if(v_b3_ch10_reason == 1) {
 "Think about what you are doing, Rose," I call out, keeping my voice low and steady as I take a slow half-step forward. "Is this what the God of Death promised you? To slaughter a child who cares about you, while Eiden stands there laughing? Throwing away your only hostage buys you nothing. There has to be another path."

"Another path..." she repeats, her dead monotone fracturing. Her grip on the dagger slackens, the trembling in her fingers spreading down her wrist. The steel edge eases back from Flower's throat. "Yes... another path."

Flower does not hesitate. She ducks beneath the revenant's unbraced arm, breaks clear across the grass, and kicks off the ground with twin torrents of crimson fire roaring from the soles of her boots, rocketing straight into the canopy well out of reach.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_reason == 0) {
 "Take that blade off her neck this second," I say, stepping forward with my hand dropping to my dagger, "or you will not survive to see the sunset."

"Your threats carry no weight," she says, her yellow eyes glazing as Death's directive locks back into place. "The will of Death is absolute. If this vessel falls, it is a necessary offering for--"

A whip of solid water snaps out from the grass behind her, wrapping like an iron shackle around her knife wrist and yanking it backward. Flower ducks under the pinned arm and vaults clear, ignition roaring from the soles of her boots as jets of fire launch her high above the pine tops.

}
 The moment Flower clears the clearing, Eiden's aura constricts around the revenant's throat like an invisible vice. A sickening pulse of kinetic pressure lifts her four feet into the air, her legs kicking at empty space.

"That reminds me," Eiden says, watching her claw at her neck as her temporal magic fails to find purchase against his aura. "There was an experiment regarding time weavers I intended to conduct years ago. I began setting it up with Rose while she lived, but something else drew my attention."

"Stop!" Kate yells, stepping forward, frost crackling wildly across both arms. "Leave her alone!"

"Though crushing your windpipe is pointless, is it not?" Eiden muses, observing the corpse writhing midair without a flicker of remorse. "Undead flesh requires no oxygen. A change of tactic, then."

He flicks his wrist. A razor crescent of compressed wind flashes from his fingertips, severing her neck in a clean, silent arc.

The world around us fractures. The sound of her head striking the turf dissolves into a dissonant, echoing hum, like voices shouting underwater. Daren's shout, Kate's fury, Eiden's casual laughter—all of it stretches and twists, blurring into the strange, disjointed haze of a timeline being violently erased.

Just as when Daren struck her down during the God of Time's ritual, the memory of her death slips away like smoke from a waking dream. When reality snaps firm again, the revenant is standing whole on the exact patch of pine needles she occupied before Eiden raised a finger. The Magium's primordial fail-safe reversed time strictly for the prophet and her killer, leaving the rest of us with nothing but the phantom ache of an unmade sequence.

"Fascinating," Eiden remarks, his pleasant smile returning as if he had merely turned a page in a book. "Theory holds that the Magium preserves its prophets until their verse is spoken, but witnessing the mechanism is another matter entirely. The weave rewound just far enough to afford you an exit. What happens if I cut you down repeatedly, I wonder? Does the ward fatigue, or does it hold indefinitely? Shall we test its limits?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Revenant4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Revenant4)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Revenant4
TEXT:

 Before Eiden can gather his aura for another strike, the revenant dissolves into the ether. Her magical signature vanishes entirely from the surrounding forest, leaving no ripple behind.

"She withdrew," Eiden says, sighing with mild, genuine disappointment. "Pity."

He slips both hands back into his pockets, his polite, closed-eyed expression settling comfortably into place.

"Farewell," he says, and space folds silently around him as he disappears from the clearing.

"Arraka, confirm it," I say, scanning the empty pines. "Is he gone, or just hovering behind a concealment ward?"

"He left," Arraka replies dryly from the amulet. "He knows better than to play invisibility parlor tricks while I am watching."

 "Her timing was absurdly precise," Daren says, lowering his greatsword and exhaling a long, ragged breath. "She needed a hold over Eiden, and she materialized the exact hour he dropped in on our camp."

"The God of Death may hold island-wide awareness like Eiden," Illuna says, Flower floating back down to the turf with her eyes burning deep sapphire. "Or our company is being monitored directly. The golden fox maintained a scrying link on us until Eiden severed it."

"No one is tracking you," Arraka snaps from the gold. "The only reason I stayed quiet about the fox was sheer boredom. That calculation has changed. I have zero interest in giving the gods a front-row seat to our plans."

 "Even so," Illuna says, her brow furrowing, "what troubles me is that Death knew of Eiden's promise to the fox regarding Flower—a detail we never possessed. Where does one uncover that caliber of intelligence?"

"Gods keep obsessive eyes on Eiden," Arraka says with a harsh snort. "He has crossed paths with their cultists and slaughtered their priestlings for six centuries. They track his movements as best they can. A creature of his strength cannot sneeze without half the magical plane taking frantic notes."

"Let us hope that is the extent of it," Illuna mutters, crossing her arms.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Condition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Condition)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Condition
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "Indeed," Eiden says. "I always do. Farewell."

He smiles, slips his hands into his pockets, and vanishes in a silent fold of displaced air.

"Arraka, confirm it," I say, scanning the empty trees. "Is he gone, or just hovering behind a concealment ward?"

"He left," Arraka replies from the amulet. "He knows better than to play invisibility parlor tricks while I am watching."

"We should prepare for the lessathi leader," Kate says, her frost fading as she turns toward the camp. "We have no idea what escort she travels with. Better to assume hostility until proven otherwise."

While Kate, Flower, and I strike the canvas tents, Hadrik helps Daren stow his heavy steel plate into his pack, as planned. We finish packing our gear just as Melindra drops out of the sky, her brow furrowed with agitation.

"I reached Meridith," she says, boots hitting the turf. "She added an extra condition before she agrees to sit down with us. She said-- wait. Where is Eiden?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "We should prepare for the lessathi leader," Kate says, her frost fading as she turns toward the camp, her face pale and guarded after seeing Rose's corpse. "We have no idea what escort she travels with. Better to assume hostility until proven otherwise."

While Kate, Flower, and I strike the canvas tents, Hadrik helps Daren stow his heavy steel plate into his expandable backpack, as planned. We finish lashing our packs just as Melindra drops out of the sky on a dying gust of wind, her brow furrowed with agitation.

"I reached Meridith," she says, boots hitting the turf. "She added an extra condition before she agrees to sit down with us. She said-- wait. Where is Eiden?"

}
 "He departed while you were aloft," Kate tells her.

"He left?" Melindra's voice spikes into a furious screech. "He couldn't spare three minutes to speak with me? Not even a word of parting? I am going to make him pay for that."

"You mentioned a condition," Hadrik prompts, adjusting the straps of his ale-pack.

"Right. Meridith demands we meet her in their primary subterranean stronghold," Melindra says, pacing the grass. "They maintain a heavy teleportation array to pull us down, but they must send a guide to lead us to a designated beacon coordinate where the array can lock onto our transceivers."

"She wants us inside their main installation?" Daren's jaw sets hard. "That sounds like a trap waiting to snap shut."

"I agree," Kate says, stepping between us. "Risking the entire group is foolish. I should go alone. Meridith asked for me specifically."

choice("Tell Kate that it's too dangerous to go there alone.", B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_b3_ch10_worry = 1)
choice("Say nothing, and wait to see how the team reacts to Kate's proposal.", B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brother, v_b3_ch10_worry = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Brother
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_worry == 1 && v_b3_ch1_kate3 == 0) {
 "You are not walking into that stronghold alone," I say, stepping into her path. "It is far too dangerous."

"I can take care of myself, Barry," Kate replies, her jaw set, dark eyes narrowing. "There is no reason to fret over me."

"I will worry about you as much as I please, thanks," I say.

Color climbs swiftly into her cheeks, bright against her fair skin. She jerks her chin aside, staring hard into the pine thicket to hide the blush.

My thoughts flash back to the quiet woods after the God of Time's ritual, when she tried to shoulder the crushing weight of the trials alone and I told her not to shut me out—that worrying about her was my choice to make.

"Barry is right," Daren cuts in, stepping up beside us. "Sending you in alone is out of the question. We can attempt to negotiate terms with the courier Meridith dispatches, but if they refuse compromise, we swallow the risk together. We have come too far to abandon the locator now."

A tendril of solidified water lashes around Kate's waist, anchoring her in place. She spins around, startled.

"Illuna, what are you doing?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_worry == 1 && v_b3_ch1_kate3 != 0) {
 "You are not walking into that stronghold alone," I say, stepping into her path. "It is far too dangerous."

"I can take care of myself, Barry," Kate replies, her jaw set, dark eyes narrowing. "There is no reason to fret over me."

"I will worry about you as much as I please, thanks," I say.

"Barry is right," Daren cuts in, stepping up beside us. "Sending you in alone is out of the question. We can attempt to negotiate terms with the courier Meridith dispatches, but if they refuse compromise, we swallow the risk together. We have come too far to abandon the locator now."

A tendril of solidified water lashes around Kate's waist, anchoring her in place. She spins around, startled.

"Illuna, what are you doing?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_worry == 0) {
 "That is not an option," Daren tells Kate firmly, shaking his head. "Either the entire company enters the stronghold, or none of us do."

"You doubt my capabilities?" Kate frowns, crossing her arms over her green cloak.

"Not your skill, but the odds," Daren replies. "Marching isolated into a subterranean fortress of potential hostiles invites disaster. Even as a united front, negotiation will be fraught."

A tendril of solidified water lashes around Kate's waist, anchoring her in place. She spins around, startled.

"Illuna, what are you doing?"

}
 "We established back in Ollendor that martyrdom is off the table," Illuna says, hauling on the water whip to reel Kate a step backward. "Did that pact slip your memory so quickly? Must I keep you leashed to prevent you from wandering into enemy traps the moment our backs are turned?"

"Fine, I yield!" Kate calls out, hauled off balance. "I retract the proposal. Release the whip."

"Hmph." The water whip dissolves into a spray of cool mist. "Do not test my patience again."

"Melindra, any guess as to who Meridith will dispatch?" Daren asks, turning toward the trees.

"None," Melindra says, brushing a pine needle from her sleeve. "Every past rendezvous brought a different escort. The extraction coordinate lies just ahead. Follow me."

"Is now an acceptable time to ask how you connected with the lessathi leadership in the first place?" Hadrik asks as we fall in behind her along the deer trail.

"My brother maintained dealings with her," Melindra says. "He facilitated our introduction centuries ago."

"If you have walked this land for six centuries and your brother still lives, does that make him a stillwater as well?" Kate asks, keeping pace.

 "Yes," Melindra says. "He and I were the only half-lessathi present when--"

She cuts herself off, catching the slip just in time.

"We were the only two half-lessathi among those who became stillwaters," she clarifies smoothly, though her eyes dart away. "The rest were human casters, Eiden included."

"And I imagine you remain as tight-lipped regarding how stillwaters are made as during our previous inquiries," Daren notes dryly.

"Naturally," Melindra says, glancing back over her shoulder. "And it serves your survival. Eiden systematically erased every scrap of documentation surrounding that process to ensure another still winter never recurs. If he suspected you held even a fragment of that truth, your lives would be forfeit on the spot."

"A fair point," Daren concedes. "Our list of mortal adversaries is long enough without adding another. Do we turn here?"

"Around this thicket, then west," Melindra directs. "Nearly there."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Designated, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Designated)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Designated
TEXT:

 When we break through the dense thicket into the small clearing, the envoy is already waiting. But she wears no lessathi robes. Instead, standing before us on the mossy rock is a young woman of twenty-three with a wiry, athletic build, a pale burn scar tracing down her left forearm, and cropped blond hair crackling with faint arcs of static electricity that lift the strands on end.

"Diane!" Kate calls out, her voice breaking with raw relief. "You're really here!"

"You never listen, do you?" Diane says, sighing with weary affection as she crosses her arms. "I explicitly told you to leave the island, yet here you are, marching straight back into--"

Kate cuts her off with a fierce, bone-crushing embrace, wrapping both arms around her before Diane can take half a step back.

"I have you," Kate says, burying her face into Diane's shoulder, her fingers bunching into the fabric of Diane's tunic. "You aren't slipping away again. Not this time."

"Hey—stop, let go!" Diane laughs, struggling halfheartedly against Kate's iron grip. "You are embarrassing me in front of your company!"

 "Good to see you again, lass!" Hadrik rumbles, stepping forward with a broad grin. "Though you caused us a mountain of grief, girl. Knocking us cold in the sacred woods left us straight in the golden fox's jaws. Nearly died three times over before breakfast!"

"You did?" Diane wriggles free of Kate's hold, her hazel eyes going wide with alarm. "I had no idea—I didn't consider--"

"Bwahaha!" Hadrik boomed, clapping a heavy hand against his knee. "Relax! Just poking at you. We came through in one piece, with grand tales to show for it. All's well that ends well!"

"Right..." Diane rubs the back of her neck, unsettled by the near-miss. "Still, I owe everyone an apology. My conduct was unacceptable. I was frantic to keep Kate away from the lessathi and reacted without thinking. Under pressure, I default to terrible decisions. An old failing I haven't managed to outgrow. Truly, I am sorry. I will make it right."

 "If keeping Kate away from lessathi hands mattered so much to you," Daren says, his brow furrowing, "why do you serve them? Are you under coercion?"

"No," Diane says, meeting his gaze squarely. "To be precise, I work alongside Meridith. Not the rest of her people."

"Is she not their ruler?" Hadrik asks.

"In title," Diane says. "Their society fractured decades ago, following Lord Heksol's death. The traditionalists avoid outright rebellion, but they actively undermine Meridith's policies at every turn. Meridith believes she can gradually reform the culture from within. I find her optimism misplaced."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Advanced, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Advanced)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Advanced
TEXT:

 "Why remain here at all?" Kate asks softly, her hand lingering on Diane's sleeve. "Why not cross the ocean and build a life? Is Jason the reason?"

"Partly," Diane admits, looking toward the canopy. "My brother idolizes Meridith. Nothing would convince him to abandon her. But beyond that, I saw firsthand what lessathi extremism creates. I will not allow another Beacon of Hope to emerge. Meridith dismantled the facility, outlawed the execution of runaways, and banned the disposal of disabled infants. She forced basic decency onto an empire that had none. I respect her for that fight, and that is why I stand with her."

"A complicated picture," Daren says, stroking his chin. "Meridith sounds reasonable, but she governs a hostile populace barely held in check. What prevents them from ambushing us the moment we materialize in their territory?"

 "Daren raises a valid concern," Kate says. "Diane, is entering their central base truly safe?"

"You need not worry," Diane replies evenly.

"Why?" Kate asks.

"Because if any of them lay a finger on you, I will slaughter them," Diane says, her voice flat and utterly devoid of boast. Her pupils suddenly flare with a blazing, incandescent gold light. "I will tear through their ranks, and they are fully aware of what I can do. They will not dare touch you."

Flower gasps, pointing directly at Diane's face with both hands.

"You have the glowing eye trick!" Flower's face lights up in pure delight. "I thought I was the only one! Watch, I can do it too!"

Flower clenches both fists, focusing her aura until heat ripples visibly off her shoulders. Her eyes ignite into a fierce, radiant crimson, tiny embers swirling within the irises.

"That... is an advanced ethereal discipline," Diane says, her breath catching as she studies the intensity of Flower's flames. "Mastering that resonance usually demands decades of dedicated training. To achieve it at your age is astonishing."

"I am thirty!" Flower protests, puffing out her chest indignantly. "I am older than you are!"

"Uh..." Diane glances between Flower and the rest of us, bewildered. "Is she pulling my leg, or...?"

choice(""Yeah, she's just joking. Don't worry about it."", B3-Ch10c-Joking, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Joking)
choice(""Actually, believe it or not, she's telling the truth."", B3-Ch10c-Truth, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Truth)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Joking
TEXT:

 "Yeah, she's just joking. Don't worry about it."

"I am not joking!" Flower shouts, stomping her boot into the dirt. "I am thirty years old!"

The crimson in her eyes vanishes, replaced instantly by glacial blue as Illuna seizes control of the expression, her mouth flattening into a sharp line.

"We had an explicit agreement regarding comments on Flower's age," Illuna says, glaring daggers at me. "If you intend to break it, I politely request you regurgitate the slice of birthday cake you accepted and return it."

"Right. My mistake," I cough into my fist, taking a half-step back.

"What was that?" Diane's hand drops toward her side in surprise. "Her eyes turned vivid blue and her entire posture shifted."

"That is Illuna, the banshee sharing her physical form," I explain. "Their souls fused two decades ago, freezing Flower's biological growth. They reside together within Flower's mind on the ethereal plane, holding daily tea and biscuits."

"A banshee..." Diane studies Flower, her brow furrowing with genuine fascination. "That accounts for the dual harmonic in her aura. But housing two distinct wills in a single mind... I had no idea such fusion was possible."

"Hate to cut in," Hadrik says, shifting his weight, "but is this the extraction point, or are we marching further?"

 "A short walk," Diane says, turning on her heel. "Follow me."

"Diane, there is so much to tell you," Kate says, falling into step beside her along the ridge. "You have no idea what has happened. I found Leila. She has been traveling with our company."

"Leila?" Diane pauses, searching her memory. "The mute girl with silver hair who fled with the caretaker? She survived? That is wonderful."

"She communicates through an enchanted interface now," Kate says, her tone warming in a way she rarely lets strangers hear. "It transcribes her thoughts into floating glyphs. We became close. I will introduce you when this is over. You will like her."

"I look forward to it," Diane smiles.

As we hike through the brush, Kate recounts the key points of her exile, her crossing back to Varathia, and our progress through the tournament brackets.

Reaching a barren outcrop of granite, Diane unclips her transceiver and relays our coordinates to the operator below. Once confirmed, she checks that we are set, then gives the signal to engage the array.

Blinding white light wraps around us, accompanied by a deep, resonant hum that vibrates through our teeth and ribs. The forest dissolves. We materialize inside a vast subterranean vault, standing before a teleportation dais similar to the rebel platform in Ollendor, but scaled considerably larger and lined with dark seredium plating. Behind the control console stands a lessathi technician in formal dark blue robes, observing us with cold scrutiny.

Standing at the edge of the chamber is Meridith herself. I recognize her instantly from the memories revealed during Kate's trial. Her chestnut hair is trimmed shorter, and silver filigree lines the hem of her robes to denote high office, but her green eyes hold the exact steel resolve she carried a decade ago.

She approaches with measured steps, halting directly before Kate.

"You must be Kate," Meridith says.

"I am," Kate answers evenly.

"I see." Meridith lets out a long, quiet breath.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Scepter, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Scepter)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Truth
TEXT:

 "Actually, believe it or not, she's telling the truth."

"She is?" Diane looks from Flower to me, her brow furrowing. "How can she be thirty? She has the frame of a nine-year-old child."

"Her soul was fused with a banshee named Illuna," I explain. "They share the body, living inside Flower's ethereal mind space where they drink tea and eat biscuits every day."

"A banshee..." Diane studies Flower, her eyes widening slightly. "That accounts for the dual harmonic in her aura. But housing two distinct wills in a single mind... I had no idea such fusion was possible."

"Hate to cut in," Hadrik says, shifting his weight, "but is this the extraction point, or are we marching further?"

 "A short walk," Diane says, turning on her heel. "Follow me."

"Diane, there is so much to tell you," Kate says, falling into step beside her along the ridge. "You have no idea what has happened. I found Leila. She has been traveling with our company."

"Leila?" Diane pauses, searching her memory. "The mute girl with silver hair who fled with the caretaker? She survived? That is wonderful."

"She communicates through an enchanted interface now," Kate says, her tone warming in a way she rarely lets strangers hear. "It transcribes her thoughts into floating glyphs. We became close. I will introduce you when this is over. You will like her."

"I look forward to it," Diane smiles.

As we hike through the brush, Kate recounts the key points of her exile, her crossing back to Varathia, and our progress through the tournament brackets.

Reaching a barren outcrop of granite, Diane unclips her transceiver and relays our coordinates to the operator below. Once confirmed, she checks that we are set, then gives the signal to engage the array.

Blinding white light wraps around us, accompanied by a deep, resonant hum that vibrates through our teeth and ribs. The forest dissolves. We materialize inside a vast subterranean vault, standing before a teleportation dais similar to the rebel platform in Ollendor, but scaled considerably larger and lined with dark seredium plating. Behind the control console stands a lessathi technician in formal dark blue robes, observing us with cold scrutiny.

Standing at the edge of the chamber is Meridith herself. I recognize her instantly from the memories revealed during Kate's trial. Her chestnut hair is trimmed shorter, and silver filigree lines the hem of her robes to denote high office, but her green eyes hold the exact steel resolve she carried a decade ago.

She approaches with measured steps, halting directly before Kate.

"You must be Kate," Meridith says.

"I am," Kate answers evenly.

"I see." Meridith lets out a long, quiet breath.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Scepter, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Scepter)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Scepter
TEXT:

 Meridith presents an ornate scepter resting across her open palms, its dark metallic shaft etched with intricate geometric bands and crowned with deep sapphire gemstones that pulse with contained force.

"This belongs to you," Meridith says, extending the weapon.

"What is it?" Kate's dark eyes narrow with instant suspicion.

"An heirloom of our civilization," Meridith says. "Preserved for over a millennium, from the zenith of the lessathi empire across Varathia. I offer it as restitution for the atrocities inflicted upon you. It cannot undo the horrors of the Beacon, but it stands as an acknowledgment of our debt."

"Sacrilege!" The teleporter engineer slams both hands down onto the console, his face twisting with rage. "Unthinkable! The sacred scepter of Heldenbusorag is not a trinket to hand out to surface vagrants! You desecrate our lineage, Lady Meridith!"

"I desecrate nothing," Meridith says, her voice low, measured, and entirely unyielding. "The weight of the relic is precisely what gives the restitution meaning. A hollow gesture would insult us both."

"I cannot take this," Kate says, stepping back a pace. "If it holds that significance to your culture, keep it in your vault."

 "Take it," Meridith says gently, closing Kate's fingers around the cool grip. "It serves no purpose gathering dust in a reliquary. In the hands of a disciplined elementalist, it sharpens magical compression to an extraordinary degree. Test it."

Kate hesitates, her fingers curling around the etched metal, then aims the crown toward the reinforced cavern wall and unleashes a lance of frost.

The icicle punches cleanly through the thick seredium reinforcement, boring a smooth cylindrical tunnel deep into the bedrock before shattering into diamond dust.

"Remarkable," Kate breathes, staring at the scepter in her hand. "Structuring an ice projectile to that density normally requires several seconds of unbroken focus."

"I am pleased it answers to your touch," Meridith says with a faint nod.

"The Heksol faction will not tolerate this, Lady Meridith," the operator snaps, his knuckles whitening on the controls. "I will report this transgression myself."

 "Save your breath, Grihirt," Meridith replies coolly, turning her back on him. "I published my decree the hour I summoned Kate here. If your circle doubted my resolve, seeing the relic in her grip will cure their skepticism. Times evolve. Heksol lies in a grave, yet you cling to his ghost. Reality demands your attention."

"We shall see," Grihirt sneers.

"Follow me," Meridith gestures down the adjoining corridor. "The walk to our command center is long."

"Lead on," Daren says, falling in behind her as we leave the fuming engineer behind his console.

"I understand demanding your presence here for the locator felt heavy-handed," Meridith says as we navigate the torchlit tunnels, "but I intend to ensure this journey rewards your effort. I cannot erase the past, but I can enforce proper compensation."

"Why expend such resources on our behalf?" Illuna asks from Flower's throat, her sapphire eyes gleaming in the torchlight. "Tournament stewards claim strict neutrality."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Secret, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Secret)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Secret
TEXT:

 "Diane's companions are under my protection. That extends to you, Illuna of the sacred woods massacre," Meridith says, not missing a beat as we walk. We exchange glances behind her, realizing she had cataloged Flower's passenger long before we stepped off the teleportation platform. "Diane's assistance over the decade since the Beacon's closure placed me heavily in her debt. I repay it where I can. If there is anything your party requires, name it."

"I provided no formal introduction," Illuna remarks, her voice suspicious. "How did you identify me?"

"I direct lessathi intelligence," Meridith replies smoothly, keeping her gaze forward. "Tracking notable figures across Varathia is standard practice. Your pair made a considerable impression over the last twenty years, and rumors tie you to the golden fox. It would be professional negligence not to know your face."

"Fair enough," Illuna says.

Meridith pauses before a blank expanse of wall and taps a specific sequence against the seredium with the tip of a slender metal rod. A concealed seam parts silently, revealing a narrow private corridor.

 "You offered assistance," I say as we step through and the heavy seredium partition seals behind us. "Does that cover restricted data? Specifically, the override cipher that unchains the magical attributes on my stat device. I understand only executive lessathi hold that sequence."

"I possess the sequence, but you misunderstand its function," Meridith says as we proceed down the torchlit passage. "The magical pathways in those units do not respond to pure lessathi biology. They activate exclusively for half-lessathi hybrids. That limitation prompted the lockdown in the first place—hybrids were deemed second-class by the council, unfit to wield such capabilities. The modules remained in production because ancient engineers six centuries ago hoped to bypass the restriction, until the still winter wiped them out. Unlocking those stats on your device yields nothing."

"I am half-lessathi," I say plainly. "I activated them during Emergency Mode. They function fine."

"Half-lessathi?" Meridith slows her pace, then stops entirely. "Impossible. Interbreeding with humans carries mandatory exile or execution under our statutes. And if you entered through the tournament registry, you grew up across the ocean. That implies your parents somehow--"

She freezes in the corridor, turning slowly to look me up and down. For the first time, the immaculate composure slips from her features.

"What is your age?" she asks, her voice dropping to a harsh whisper.

"Twenty-eight," I answer.

"Twenty-eight years..." Her eyes unfocus, running dates through decades of memory. "Twenty-eight years since--"

She trails off, her breath catching in her throat.

"No. That cannot be."

She raises the slender metal rod, taps its base to her left shoulder, then reaches out and touches the opposite tip to mine. The metal resonates with a low, harmonic chime, suddenly flaring with a deep, vibrant emerald luminescence that paints our faces green. Meridith stares at the glowing rod, her fingers trembling against the grip.

"The harmonic is identical," she whispers into the silence.

"Meaning what?" I ask, my pulse thumping against my ribs.

"Meaning we share blood," Meridith says, her eyes lifting to mine.

choice(""Wait, so does that make me a lessathi prince?"", B3-Ch10c-Prince, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Prince)
choice(""I thought my real parents were supposed to have fled Varathia before I was born."", B3-Ch10c-Fled, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Fled)
choice(""So am I supposed to call you Mother, or do we skip the family reunion?"", B3-Ch10c-Mommy, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Mommy)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Prince
TEXT:

 "Wait, so does that make me a lessathi prince?"

"Our society functions on meritocracy, not hereditary entitlement," Meridith says sharply, though the edge in her voice is blunted by shock. "Leadership is earned through achievement. Furthermore, I am your aunt, not your mother. My sister bore you."

"Does this mean you know the fate of my biological parents?" I ask, the words tight in my throat.

"Yes." Meridith dips her chin, a shadow crossing her face. "They were executed on Lord Heksol's order for conceiving a hybrid child and fleeing Varathia without authorization."

"How did they get off the island?" I ask. "I thought intercontinental teleportation was restricted to the highest command."

"I assisted their escape," Meridith says. "I served as Heksol's executive assistant at the time, which granted me access to the activation keys in his private archives. I acquired the coordinates, bribed a perimeter guard to stage a diversion, and saw them through the array with my own hands."

"And you survived to become leader?" I ask. "How did you avoid an execution cell?"

 "Heksol suspected my involvement immediately, but lacked definitive evidence," Meridith says, her voice steadying as she recounts the years. "My standing within the ranks was already established, preventing him from acting on assumption alone. He stripped me of my clerical post and banished me to logistical oversight. Over the following decades, I advanced through competence and unassailable results until he had no choice but to appoint me his second."

She pauses, looking down at the emerald glow fading from the resonance wand.

"I failed to search for you," Meridith says quietly. "When my sister departed, we severed all communication to ensure her location could never be extracted from me under torture. I uncovered the execution reports years later, filed away in Heksol's legacy archives after his demise. I possessed no name, no continent, no certainty you survived infancy. My only recourse was carrying this resonance rod on diplomatic crossings, hoping against hope for an emerald signal. To have you materialize here, unbidden..."

"What happens now?" I ask, the weight of twenty-eight years settling between us. "Do we embrace?"

 "No," Meridith says, the executive steel snapping back over her posture as she pockets the rod. "Though your arrival is remarkably opportune. There is someone essential you must meet. I intended to bring Kate before him, but your lineage makes your presence equally vital."

"Who?" I ask. "Another lessathi officer?"

"You will see shortly," Meridith says, turning down the passage. "We have no time to squander."

As we follow her through the quiet corridors, a chill ripples across my shoulders. Eiden's parting words ring clear in my memory:

"Aren't you pleased, Barry? Now you finally have the opportunity to deliver my message."

Eiden's message.

'Remember the still winter.' Phrased like a direct threat. How does Meridith take those words? Imprisonment? Retaliation? Or is it something else? Stillwaters fought lessathi six centuries ago, but Melindra claimed Eiden took the lessathi side. Perhaps it serves as a warning rather than a challenge.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Phrasing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Phrasing)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Fled
TEXT:

 "I thought my real parents were supposed to have fled Varathia before I was born."

"They did," Meridith says, her gaze searching my face. "My sister crossed the ocean carrying you in her womb. I am your aunt, not your mother."

"Does this mean you know the fate of my biological parents?" I ask, the words tight in my throat.

"Yes." Meridith dips her chin, a shadow crossing her face. "They were executed on Lord Heksol's order for conceiving a hybrid child and fleeing Varathia without authorization."

"How did they get off the island?" I ask. "I thought intercontinental teleportation was restricted to the highest command."

"I assisted their escape," Meridith says. "I served as Heksol's executive assistant at the time, which granted me access to the activation keys in his private archives. I acquired the coordinates, bribed a perimeter guard to stage a diversion, and saw them through the array with my own hands."

"And you survived to become leader?" I ask. "How did you avoid an execution cell?"

 "Heksol suspected my involvement immediately, but lacked definitive evidence," Meridith says, her voice steadying as she recounts the years. "My standing within the ranks was already established, preventing him from acting on assumption alone. He stripped me of my clerical post and banished me to logistical oversight. Over the following decades, I advanced through competence and unassailable results until he had no choice but to appoint me his second."

She pauses, looking down at the emerald glow fading from the resonance wand.

"I failed to search for you," Meridith says quietly. "When my sister departed, we severed all communication to ensure her location could never be extracted from me under torture. I uncovered the execution reports years later, filed away in Heksol's legacy archives after his demise. I possessed no name, no continent, no certainty you survived infancy. My only recourse was carrying this resonance rod on diplomatic crossings, hoping against hope for an emerald signal. To have you materialize here, unbidden..."

"What happens now?" I ask, the weight of twenty-eight years settling between us. "Do we embrace?"

 "No," Meridith says, the executive steel snapping back over her posture as she pockets the rod. "Though your arrival is remarkably opportune. There is someone essential you must meet. I intended to bring Kate before him, but your lineage makes your presence equally vital."

"Who?" I ask. "Another lessathi officer?"

"You will see shortly," Meridith says, turning down the passage. "We have no time to squander."

As we follow her through the quiet corridors, a chill ripples across my shoulders. Eiden's parting words ring clear in my memory:

"Aren't you pleased, Barry? Now you finally have the opportunity to deliver my message."

Eiden's message.

'Remember the still winter.' Phrased like a direct threat. How does Meridith take those words? Imprisonment? Retaliation? Or is it something else? Stillwaters fought lessathi six centuries ago, but Melindra claimed Eiden took the lessathi side. Perhaps it serves as a warning rather than a challenge.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Phrasing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Phrasing)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Mommy
TEXT:

 "So am I supposed to call you Mother, or do we skip the family reunion?"

"I suspect you are mocking me, but no, I am not your mother," Meridith says, her sharp reply cutting through the shock. "You are my sister's son, which makes me your aunt."

"Does this mean you know the fate of my biological parents?" I ask, the sarcasm dropping away as the question lands.

"Yes." Meridith dips her chin, a shadow crossing her face. "They were executed on Lord Heksol's order for conceiving a hybrid child and fleeing Varathia without authorization."

"How did they get off the island?" I ask. "I thought intercontinental teleportation was restricted to the highest command."

"I assisted their escape," Meridith says. "I served as Heksol's executive assistant at the time, which granted me access to the activation keys in his private archives. I acquired the coordinates, bribed a perimeter guard to stage a diversion, and saw them through the array with my own hands."

"And you survived to become leader?" I ask. "How did you avoid an execution cell?"

 "Heksol suspected my involvement immediately, but lacked definitive evidence," Meridith says, her voice steadying as she recounts the years. "My standing within the ranks was already established, preventing him from acting on assumption alone. He stripped me of my clerical post and banished me to logistical oversight. Over the following decades, I advanced through competence and unassailable results until he had no choice but to appoint me his second."

She pauses, looking down at the emerald glow fading from the resonance wand.

"I failed to search for you," Meridith says quietly. "When my sister departed, we severed all communication to ensure her location could never be extracted from me under torture. I uncovered the execution reports years later, filed away in Heksol's legacy archives after his demise. I possessed no name, no continent, no certainty you survived infancy. My only recourse was carrying this resonance rod on diplomatic crossings, hoping against hope for an emerald signal. To have you materialize here, unbidden..."

"What happens now?" I ask, the weight of twenty-eight years settling between us. "Do we embrace?"

 "No," Meridith says, the executive steel snapping back over her posture as she pockets the rod. "Though your arrival is remarkably opportune. There is someone essential you must meet. I intended to bring Kate before him, but your lineage makes your presence equally vital."

"Who?" I ask. "Another lessathi officer?"

"You will see shortly," Meridith says, turning down the passage. "We have no time to squander."

As we follow her through the quiet corridors, a chill ripples across my shoulders. Eiden's parting words ring clear in my memory:

"Aren't you pleased, Barry? Now you finally have the opportunity to deliver my message."

Eiden's message.

'Remember the still winter.' Phrased like a direct threat. How does Meridith take those words? Imprisonment? Retaliation? Or is it something else? Stillwaters fought lessathi six centuries ago, but Melindra claimed Eiden took the lessathi side. Perhaps it serves as a warning rather than a challenge.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Phrasing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Phrasing)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Phrasing
TEXT:

 Ignoring Eiden's instructions is not an option. The last time I neglected his courier duty, he nearly tore us apart in the street. I have no desire to test his wrath twice.

"Meridith," I call out as we march through the gloom. "This is abrupt, but I carry a message for you. From Eiden."

"From Eiden?" Meridith stops in her tracks. She does not turn around. "What words could he have for me?"

"Remember the still winter."

Silence falls heavy in the corridor, suffocating and cold. She stands completely motionless.

"That was all," I add into the quiet. "He said you would understand the meaning."

"You are certain of the phrasing?" she asks, resuming her stride with slow, calculated steps. "Word for word?"

"Word for word," I say, keeping pace.

"Did he direct this to me specifically?"

 "Not originally," I admit. "I was instructed to deliver it to the first lessathi I encountered. I was half-dead at the time and failed to pass it along. Eiden took offense when he realized I had crossed paths with your people in Ollendor and said nothing, so I made sure to discharge the debt now."

"How did he know you had met lessathi?" Meridith asks, her head tilting. "Did you report the encounter?"

"He read it off us," I say. "His magical perception covers the entire island. He detected the lessathi resonance lingering on our auras."

"What did you say?" Meridith whips around, staring directly into my eyes, her face stark with alarm.

"About what?"

"You claim Eiden identified lessathi operatives simply by scanning their aura signatures from across the continent?"

 "Yes," I say. "His range is absurd."

"You do not grasp the implication," Meridith says, her composure cracking. "Every operative on the surface wears an enchanted band beneath their sleeve to mask their presence. If Eiden cuts through that shielding at distance, our field agents are completely exposed."

"Gwahaha!" Arraka cackles from the amulet. "You believed those cheap copper bands would blind Eiden? You would need to amplify their density a hundredfold to baffle a stillwater of his caliber!"

"A hundredfold..." Meridith absorbs the mockery without flinching, already calculating numbers in her head. "We can cannibalize redundant units and consolidate the warding cores. It will leave fewer scouts equipped, but those deployed will remain hidden. I will assign the workshop to reconfigure the prototypes immediately."

"Good luck with that," Arraka snorts.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Deserved, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Deserved)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Deserved
TEXT:

 "I don't understand why Eiden's tracking concerns you so deeply," I say as we push farther down the shaft. "Was he not an ally to the lessathi during the still winter?"

"An ally?" Meridith's tone hardens instantly, ice beneath the words. "Eiden was the spark that ignited the slaughter. Am I expected to bow in gratitude because he switched allegiances in the eleventh hour, after his brethren had butchered the bulk of our civilization? The still winter was no war. It was extermination, and every stillwater who drew breath carries that butcher's stain."

"Hold on," Melindra cuts in, her voice flaring with indignation. "Neither I, nor my brother, nor Eiden took part in that slaughter. And your people invited that catastrophe. If the lessathi had not caged Eiden and the other casters like cattle, events would never have erupted into--"

"Our fault?" Meridith wheels on her, fury cold and sharp in her green eyes. "The women? The infants in their cradles? You claim they earned their slaughter?"

 "I--" Melindra falters, the words sticking in her throat as she looks away. "I do not endorse what the stillwaters did. I am stating they were not the instigators."

"Hold up," I interject. "Were those casters imprisoned because lessathi researchers were experimenting on them to manufacture stillwaters? Is that what triggered the collapse?"

"None hold the truth of their creation save the stillwaters themselves," Meridith says, casting a disdainful look at Melindra. "Eiden ensured the methodology was obliterated so no human could ever rise to challenge his supremacy."

"So the recipe is lost to you as well," I murmur. "Interesting."

 Meridith taps the end of the shaft with her rod. Another concealed seam divides, ushering us into a corridor blazing with sapphire radiance. Blue crystals line the stone from floor to ceiling, their jagged facets catching the torchlight. As in Ollendor's deep caverns, the minerals pulse with a slow, rhythmic cadence, though the thrum feels less volatile than four days ago.

"That is a massive concentration of crystals," Hadrik remarks as the stone seals behind us.

"Indeed," Meridith says, turning down the right fork where the clusters thicken. "If the pulsation alarms you, they have maintained this harmonic for four days without incident. We continue to investigate the resonance trigger, without breakthrough."

choice("Tell Meridith that everything began after the king of Ollendor blew up the caves.", B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_b3_ch10_crystals = 1)
choice("Don't tell Meridith anything about the crystals.", B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Brightly, v_b3_ch10_crystals = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Brightly
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_crystals == 1) {
 "We know the trigger," I say, glancing at the glowing formations. "The resonance began when the king of Ollendor detonated the deep caverns beneath the city to bury the rebels."

"You claim these minerals reacted to an explosion across the continent?" Meridith asks, her eyes narrowing as she studies my face.

"The pattern matches," I say. "The Ollendor deposits fell into the exact same rhythm—a slow dimming and flaring, like a collective heartbeat."

"If verified, that correlation will advance our research considerably," Meridith says. "I appreciate the disclosure."

"Glad to help," I say.

"We have not encountered a single lessathi since the teleportation room," Kate observes, her eyes scanning the sapphire-lit tunnel.

"These thoroughfares remain restricted to executive ranks," Meridith says, leading us forward. "Transit requires my direct authorization."

"A council chamber, then?" Daren asks.

"No," Meridith says quietly. "A consecrated sanctum."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_crystals == 0) {
 I keep the Ollendor detonation to myself. Handing over critical tactical intelligence before understanding her motives is bad practice.

"We have not encountered a single lessathi since the teleportation room," Kate observes, her eyes scanning the sapphire-lit tunnel.

"These thoroughfares remain restricted to executive ranks," Meridith says, leading us forward. "Transit requires my direct authorization."

"A council chamber, then?" Daren asks.

"No," Meridith says quietly. "A consecrated sanctum."

}
 She halts before an unadorned seredium partition, free of crystal growth, and turns to face us.

"We have arrived," Meridith says. "The entity awaiting your presence resides beyond this threshold."

"You are finally going to name our host?" Hadrik asks, resting a thumb in his belt.

"Yes," Meridith says, raising her wand to the cold metal surface. "A presence you know by legend, though few living souls have stood before it."

The wall responds to the wand's contact with a low, hydraulic groan, the heavy seredium slab retracting smoothly into the ceiling.

"The ultimate consciousness," Meridith intones as the chamber opens before us. "The guide of our people since antiquity."

Within the vast, cavernous vault sits a colossal sphere of solid blue radiance, churning endlessly around a central core of impenetrable darkness.

"Behold our true Creator," Meridith says, bowing her head. "The Magium."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Panicked, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Panicked)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Panicked
TEXT:

 "Run!" Arraka screeches, her voice cracking with pure panic inside the golden amulet. "Get back, you morons! Run before it--"

A blinding wave of sapphire luminescence erupts from the sphere, surging across the stone floor in a silent flash and swallowing us whole.

The cavern vanishes instantly. Weight, gravity, direction, and breath cease to exist. We drift suspended in an unmapped void, shapes without contours, my companions turning in blind confusion. Before my mind can anchor itself, the fabric of space shreds, and I plummet into the absolute nothingness of the void—the same terrifying erasure where the Magium hurled me before.

Cold terror grips my consciousness. I blink violently in and out of reality, ripped between complete nonexistence and the Magium's overwhelming conceptual presence. With every pulse, my companions reappear beside me, drenched in cold sweat, eyes wide with horror as their minds buckle against the erasure.

"Barry..." Kate's voice reaches me, thin, trembling, and fragile. "Is this the void you spoke of? I heard your description, but... this is worse than any nightmare..."

"Arraka, move!" Daren shouts, fighting to keep his voice from breaking under the pressure. "Cast the primordial ward!"

 "I cannot!" Arraka screams from the amulet, her voice shrill with terror. "The spell finds no purchase! There is no entity to target! It is everywhere and nowhere!"

The void tears at my consciousness again, a violent undertow pulling my existence toward permanent dissolution.

"Magium, stop!" Flower cries out, tears of terror in her voice. "Petal and I helped you before! We can help again! Tell us what you need! Please!"

The violent oscillation ceases. We float suspended in the impossible geometry of the Magium's realm. Space refuses definition; perspective bends in dimensions that have no names. Time bleeds forward and backward simultaneously—I perceive our arrival as a single instant, yet remember it as an ancient, repeating cycle unfolding in reverse.

The entity answers Flower, its tone shifting with each syllable, weaving through a terrifying chorus of disparate voices.

"In the past, you offered aid," speaks the dry, raspy voice of an aged man.

"In the present, you have failed," continues a woman's measured, icy cadence.

"The prophecy has been spoken," says an adolescent youth.

"You understand what must be done," chimes a young girl.

"We don't!" Flower insists frantically, her small hands clutching empty air. "We don't understand! We haven't even identified the two ancient adversaries clashing! We assume Eiden is one, because he made it obvious, but the other--"

"Then you hold your answer," a woman's soft murmur cuts in.

"To preserve the multitude, a single life must be forfeit," speaks the halting, innocent voice of a small child.

"A sacrifice?" Flower whispers, horror dawning on her face. "You... you demand we execute Eiden?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Guidance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Guidance)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Guidance
TEXT:

 "The prophecy must not come to pass," a man's voice rings out, sharp, harsh, and absolute.

"If you require direction, the one named Meridith will instruct you," an elderly woman speaks gently.

"Do not fail again!" an old man roars, shaking the void.

Reality snaps shut like an iron trap. In the fraction of a heartbeat, the Magium expels us from its domain, dropping us hard onto the cold stone floor before the rotating sphere of blue light. Everyone stumbles, gasping for air, shaking off the crushing disorientation.

"Astonishing," Meridith breathes, watching us with rapt fascination from the threshold. "I have communed with our Creator on numerous occasions, yet he never granted me audience within his personal realm. Your eyes flared with brilliant luminescence, exactly as recorded in ancient chronicles."

"Right..." Daren steadies his footing, wiping cold sweat from his brow. "You had no foreknowledge of this?"

 "None," Meridith says, her gaze lingering on our auras. "You require privacy to process an encounter of this magnitude. I will grant you several minutes while I arrange the council chamber down the adjoining hall. When you conclude your communion, close the barrier by tapping the seredium five times. I await you at the corridor's end. Take whatever time you need."

"Understood," Daren mutters, resting a hand on his hilt. "We will take our time."

"Diane, accompany me to prepare the chamber," Meridith instructs, turning on her heel.

"Right away," Diane says, throwing a quick, concerned wave over her shoulder as she follows Meridith into the passage. "See you shortly."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Scattered, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Scattered)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Scattered
TEXT:

 As Meridith and Diane depart down the hall, the others lean against the seredium walls, drawing ragged breaths to steady their racing pulses. Meanwhile, my attention pulls inexorably back toward the rotating sphere. An invisible gravity tugs at my thoughts.

The ultimate objective of the entire tournament sits three paces from me. Walk away now, and the chance to understand it may never return.

"A Magium node buried this deep," Arraka's voice mutters from the amulet, shaken but recovering her venom. "Sloppy. I allowed the seredium shielding to blind my magical sense."

"What is a Magium node?" Hadrik asks, rubbing his temples. "The floating light construct?"

"A focal point," Arraka explains. "Several exist across Varathia, linked through the underlying planar network. Major factions hold them in balance. It stands to reason the lessathi secured one of their own, however carefully they concealed it."

 "Which factions?" Daren asks. "Tyrath? Eiden? The golden fox?"

"Those three, plus the reigning monarchs and Fyron," Arraka says. "A single node provides complete access to the Magium, so distribution remained stable without territorial wars. Furthermore, their locations are--"

"Barry, step away from that!" Daren shouts, noticing my approach.

I ignore him, moving closer until the sapphire radiance washes warm over my skin. Up close, the construct towers over me. The core is an absolute, lightless void, while solid sapphire bands orbit in perpetual, silent motion, casting brilliant illumination without dispelling the central dark.

 The structure commands total focus, pulling at my senses until the surrounding cavern fades into background noise.

"Barry!" Daren's voice cracks with urgency. "Do not do something catastrophic! Back up slowly!"

"He is standing on the threshold!" Arraka's laughter rings out from the gold. "Touch it, weakling! I dare you!"

choice("Touch the Magium node.", B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_b3_ch10_node = 1)
choice("Back away from the Magium node.", B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Colors, v_b3_ch10_node = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Colors
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
 I reach out and press my bare palm directly against the rotating sphere of light and void.

A tidal wave of billions of voices, concepts, geometries, and visions crashes into my mind simultaneously. For a single suspended heartbeat, the comprehensive blueprint of reality unfolds before me. Yet the instant I reach to grasp comprehension, the knowledge recedes into infinite distance, remaining unreachable however far my consciousness stretches.

I yank my hand back before the immense pressure fractures my sanity. The flood of universal mechanics evaporates as quickly as it arrived.

Yet a profound shift remains. The raw data has fled, but its foundation lingers—a permanent imprint burned into the bedrock of my memory, abandoned when the connection severed.

If I could decipher even a fraction of that residue...

"Are you completely out of your mind?" Daren grabs my tunic, hauling me bodily backward out of the chamber. "Barry, you will put me in an early grave!"

 He strikes the seredium wall five times in rapid succession. The heavy door grinds shut, sealing the node behind solid metal.

"Well?" Daren demands, catching his breath as I stand motionless. "What happened when you touched it?"

"A fraction of a second," I say, reassembling my scattered thoughts. "I touched the node and perceived the entire architecture of existence. But holding on would have dissolved my mind. Real madness, not a figure of speech. The information was laid out, but processing it would demand an eternity. Releasing it was the only way to avoid cerebral collapse."

"Remarkable restraint on your part," Daren remarks, his eyebrows raised.

"I have survival instincts," I retort.

"Good to know," Daren says. "Did you retain anything useful?"

"Hard to tell," I say. "Fragments remain, but decoding them will take time."

"Nothing immediate, then," Daren concludes.

 "A question," Kate says, glancing uneasily at the sealed partition. "Can the entity monitor our words through the red sphere with the seredium door closed?"

"Doubtful," Arraka says. "Though putting distance between us and the threshold is wise."

"Red sphere?" Hadrik interrupts, frowning as we move down the corridor. "What red sphere? The construct was yellow and black. Where did you find red?"

"The light was green," Daren says. "Perception must vary by observer. Correct, Arraka?"

"Accurate," Arraka confirms. "Flower and Illuna perceive violet. I have possessed enough vessels to observe every hue on the spectrum."

"Yet the crystal formations are blue to everyone," Kate points out. "If they are crystallized Magium essence, why does their color not shift?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 0) {
 I step backward, letting my hand fall to my side. Poking an omnipotent planar construct before hearing Meridith's proposal is an unnecessary gamble.

"Thank the gods," Daren exhales, letting out a long breath. "I thought you were going to grab it."

"The temptation was there," I say, "but testing an unknown cosmic artifact without preparation is reckless."

"Coward," Arraka snorts from the amulet.

Daren strikes the seredium partition five times, and the heavy slab grinds shut, cutting off the radiance.

"Can the entity monitor our words through the red sphere with the seredium door closed?" Kate asks, looking at the metal seam.

"Doubtful," Arraka says. "Though putting distance between us and the threshold is wise."

 "Red sphere?" Hadrik interrupts, frowning as we move down the corridor. "What red sphere? The construct was yellow and black. Where did you find red?"

"The light was green," Daren says. "Perception must vary by observer. Correct, Arraka?"

"Accurate," Arraka confirms. "Flower and Illuna perceive violet. I have possessed enough vessels to observe every hue on the spectrum."

"Yet the crystal formations are blue to everyone," Kate points out. "If they are crystallized Magium essence, why does their color not shift?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Common, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Common)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Common
TEXT:

 "The crystal mechanics are obscure," Arraka says, "but blue remains the most frequent baseline for node perception. Among the animals I inhabited over the centuries, blue manifested far more than any other shade."

"Fitting, since blue is what I saw," I remark.

"A common color for an ordinary fellow," Hadrik chuckles, giving my shoulder a solid clap. "The cosmos has a sense of humor."

"I have made peace with it," I shrug.

"Can we address what occurred in that realm?" Daren asks, glancing back at the group. "I have no desire to reopen wounds, but ignoring the demand is dangerous."

"It is straightforward," Kate says, her arms folded tightly under her cloak. "Barry prepared us for the void, which blunted the shock. But the substance of the encounter is clear. The Magium verified Eiden as one of the two figures in the prophecy and directed us to eliminate him."

"So we become assassins on command?" Hadrik asks, shaking his head. "What hold does that sphere have over us if we simply avoid its chambers?"

 "Selkram's trials link you directly to the Magium via the time weaver," Arraka explains. "During prophetic transmission, you stand upon its planar threshold. Avoiding the nodes does not eliminate exposure."

"I am no one's lackey," Hadrik rumbles, his jaw hardening. "If the Magium wishes to erase me, let it try. If circumstances force a confrontation with Eiden, fine. But I will not hunt a man because a floating light ordered it."

"Spoken like a champion," Arraka jeers. "I will ensure blankets reach your cell in the void. It grows drafty during eternity."

"Skip the blankets and send dwarven stout," Hadrik says. "Better insulation."

"Noted," Arraka laughs.

#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
 "I understand something critical," I say, stepping between them after running the impressions through my thoughts.

"Do enlighten us," Arraka says dryly.

"When I touched the node, the connection went deeper than the ritual link," I say. "It was not simply a conduit to magical power. It was union with the foundation. The source."

"The source of magic?" Daren asks.

"Not just magic," I say, the concept clear in my mind. "The source of the physical realm, the magical plane, the ethereal plane. Every parallel divergence. Time, geometry, consciousness, and volition. The source of everything."

"You claim that is the true definition of the Magium?" Kate asks. "Not the root of magic, but the origin of all existence?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Informed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Informed)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Informed
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch10_forbidden,1) if (v_b3_ch10_node == 1)
achievement("Forbidden knowledge",v_ac_b3_ch10_forbidden)
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
 "Gwahahaha!" Arraka's laughter explodes through the tunnel. "Precisely. 'Source of everything.' Those were the exact three words I withheld, because speaking them aloud reveals its nature. Since neither of you has vanished into nonexistence, we may discuss the concept openly—provided we stay clear of active nodes and Selkram's rituals. Though taking this long to deduce the obvious is embarrassing."

"The behavior of these fragments makes little sense," Daren says. "If their mandate is guarding that secret, why did the entity release us? Arraka nearly disclosed the truth in its presence before."

 "Fragments operate under isolated directives," Arraka says. "Each pursues a singular objective, blind to the rest. This entity is tasked with suppressing the prophecy. Lacking the ability to manifest physically without tearing reality apart—like the fragment driving the Overseer—it manipulates mortal agents. The entity that targeted me during Selkram's ritual was a localized defense mechanism guarding the threshold."

"Then this fragment held no awareness of your prior near-disclosure?" Hadrik asks.

"They do not communicate," Arraka says. "It attacked purely to enforce obedience regarding the prophecy. That marks the third fragment to threaten our erasure. Our mortal lives mean nothing to them."

"A single sacrifice to preserve the multitude," Daren quotes quietly. "Collateral damage."

 "Indeed," Arraka says. "And the other fragment would have unmade the world to secure my past self. These entities crush whatever obstructs their objective. Avoiding their gaze remains our best course."

"The council room lies ahead," Kate says, pointing toward an arched doorway of dark seredium. "Shall we enter?"

"We must decide whether to disclose the Magium's command to Meridith," Daren says under his breath.

"You mean the order to murder Eiden?" Melindra whispers sharply. "You cannot seriously entertain that. He will slaughter you."

"We are deciding our stance for the meeting," Daren clarifies. "Hearing her position first may guide us, or we allow her to open the subject. Thoughts?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 0) {
 "I feel as though a vital truth slipped through my fingers," I mutter, looking down at my open palm. "As though the definition of the Magium was within reach, and I stepped back."

"Doubtless," Arraka mocks. "Had you shown spine and grasped the node, the secret would be yours. Now you carry that ignorance to the grave."

"Ignore her," Daren tells me gently. "You chose prudence. An uncontrolled surge could have destroyed your mind."

"Right," I say. "Prudence."

We reach the reinforced portal at the corridor's end. Daren pauses with his hand near the handle.

"We must decide whether to disclose the Magium's command to Meridith," Daren says under his breath.

"You mean the order to murder Eiden?" Melindra whispers sharply. "You cannot seriously entertain that. He will slaughter you."

"We are deciding our stance for the meeting," Daren clarifies. "Hearing her position first may guide us, or we allow her to open the subject. Thoughts?"

}
choice(""I think it would be better to mention it to Meridith."", B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_b3_ch10_mention = 1)
choice(""We should probably avoid mentioning any of this to Meridith, for now."", B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Decorations, v_b3_ch10_mention = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Decorations
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_mention == 1) {
 "I think it would be better to mention it to Meridith," I say. "Meridith likely knows the fragment's directive already. Concealing it might raise unnecessary suspicion."

"Agreed," Daren says, his hand on the iron latch. "The Magium instructed us to seek her guidance. Does anyone object?"

"Makes no difference to me," Hadrik shrugs. "I am sitting out the manhunt regardless."

"Fine by me," Kate says, and Flower nods in agreement.

"Then we proceed," Daren says, pushing the heavy reinforced door inward.

We file into a chamber reminiscent of the underground king's audience hall in Ollendor, though the stonework here is far more refined, bearing intricate lessathi reliefs of the heraldic hawk clutching a radiant sun.

A long table of polished black stone dominates the center of the room, flanked by high-backed chairs. Meridith and Diane sit on the far side, watching our entrance. Meridith gestures toward the vacant seats opposite them, and we take our places across the cold surface.

Once we settle, Meridith leans forward, resting her forearms on the table.

 "You wonder why I brought you to this installation," Meridith says. "Answers will follow, but I must ask a preliminary question. You are under no compulsion to respond, and forgive the directness, but what transpired during your communion with our Creator?"

We exchange brief glances. Daren leans forward to speak for the table.

"The Magium instructed us to eliminate Eiden, to prevent the catastrophe foretold in the prophecy," Daren says, his voice level.

"Then my hypothesis was accurate," Meridith says, a sharp satisfaction entering her tone. "He invited you to our cause."

"Your cause?" Daren asks, brow furrowing. "You received the same mandate?"

"I did," Meridith says. "And I have directed our resources toward that objective for years."

"Before we proceed," I cut in, leaning back in my chair, "I have a question of my own."

"Ask," Meridith invites, her gaze shifting to me. "Nothing is withheld."

"Nothing?" I meet her green eyes across the table. "In that case, confirm whether you orchestrated the continent-wide buyout of stat devices prior to the tournament."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_mention == 0) {
 "Keep the Magium's command to ourselves for now," I say. "Volunteering that detail might suggest we have already signed on to the assassination. I want to hear her pitch first."

"Sensible," Daren agrees, his hand on the iron latch. "Any dissent, or do we follow Barry's lead?"

"Makes no difference to me," Hadrik shrugs. "I am sitting out the manhunt regardless."

"Fine by me," Kate says, and Flower nods in agreement.

"Then we proceed," Daren says, pushing the heavy reinforced door inward.

We file into a chamber reminiscent of the underground king's audience hall in Ollendor, though the stonework here is far more refined, bearing intricate lessathi reliefs of the heraldic hawk clutching a radiant sun.

A long table of polished black stone dominates the center of the room, flanked by high-backed chairs. Meridith and Diane sit on the far side, watching our entrance. Meridith gestures toward the vacant seats opposite them, and we take our places across the cold surface.

Once we settle, Meridith leans forward, resting her forearms on the table.

 "You wonder why I brought you to this installation," Meridith says. "Answers will follow, but I must ask a preliminary question. You are under no compulsion to respond, and forgive the directness, but what transpired during your communion with our Creator?"

We exchange brief glances. Daren answers smoothly without hesitation.

"We prefer to keep that confidential for the moment," he says.

"Understood," Meridith says without missing a beat. "Take your time. If you choose to discuss it later, my door remains open."

"Thank you," Daren says.

"Before we proceed," I cut in, leaning back in my chair, "I have a question of my own."

"Ask," Meridith invites, her gaze shifting to me. "Nothing is withheld."

"Nothing?" I meet her green eyes across the table. "In that case, confirm whether you orchestrated the continent-wide buyout of stat devices prior to the tournament."

}
 Silence hangs heavy over the table. Meridith holds my gaze, her expression completely unreadable.

"What led you to that deduction?" she asks, her voice steady and controlled.

"First," I say, "whoever organized the buyout needed immense capital, recognized the units were far more than fortune-teller novelties, and required them before the tournament commenced. The list of candidates matching that profile on this island is short."

She does not blink or shift her posture.

"Continue," she says. "What else supports your hypothesis?"

"Energy draw," I say. "My stat booster harvests significantly less charge inside Thilias and Ollendor than out in the wilderness. Furthermore, another participant who remained static in Thilias accumulated far fewer points than I did. If thousands of harvesting devices were saturated beneath those cities, the planar distribution drop makes complete sense."

"What are you implying?" Meridith asks.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Sham, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Sham)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Sham
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch7_overheard == 0) {
 "What I am stating plainly," I say, "is that this tournament is an elaborate fiction. You never intended to award the Magium to a victor. You dangled it because nothing else on this earth could lure hundreds of foreign mages into one territory. The contest is not a defense trial for Varathia. It is a harvesting net, using converted stat boosters to siphon raw magical energy from every competitor who set foot on this island."

"The delay," Hadrik grunts, his eyes narrowing as he leans forward. "That week we sat in Thilias doing nothing. The pinecone hunt across the wilderness. Stalling for time so the machines could drain us dry."

"Exactly," I say. "The only detail that eludes me is why you allowed contestants to slaughter one another in the field. Enforce a ceasefire before the opening round, and you could have siphoned them indefinitely."

 Meridith listens without a flicker of surprise, resting her chin on linked fingers. When she speaks, her voice carries the calm authority of an executive reciting minutes from a concluded session.

"That was our initial architecture," she says smoothly. "We installed the primary extractor arrays beneath the continental capitals, calculating that the majority of foreign casters would congregate inside the city walls while awaiting instructions. We disabled the central telepathic relay ourselves to stretch the waiting window. The flaw was the monarchs. The kings of Varathia rejected a non-lethal mandate, declaring bloodshed an inviolable tournament tradition—and delivered that refusal hours before the opening bell. You cannot fathom the logistical disruption that caused."

"You admit this?" Daren stares at her, bewildered. "Without a shred of hesitation?"

"Why maintain a pretense?" Meridith counters, her eyes locking onto mine. "Your companion dismantled the puzzle. Feigning ignorance wastes time, and I have far more productive uses for this audience."

}
#if(v_b2_ch7_overheard == 1) {
 "What I am stating plainly," I say, "is that this tournament is an elaborate fiction. You never intended to award the Magium to a victor. You dangled it because nothing else on this earth could lure hundreds of foreign mages into one territory. The contest is not a defense trial for Varathia. It is a harvesting net, using converted stat boosters to siphon raw magical energy from every competitor who set foot on this island."

"The delay," Hadrik grunts, his eyes narrowing as he leans forward. "That week we sat in Thilias doing nothing. The pinecone hunt across the wilderness. Stalling for time so the machines could drain us dry."

"Exactly," I say. "Your operatives in Thilias were the ones who sabotaged the telepathic relay to buy time. I heard one complaining that the kings refused a non-lethal rule. You needed participants breathing so your machines could harvest maximum charge."

Meridith's gaze sharpens. "The Thilias outpost? Sloppy. I expected better discretion from field operatives."

 "They spoke archaic lessathi in an empty thoroughfare," I say. "They had no reason to suspect a tournament participant held enhanced hearing and ancient dialects on his booster."

"Did you just confirm his deductions?" Daren asks, incredulous. "You orchestrated the entire fraud?"

"I did," Meridith says, her posture unyielding. "Denial serves no purpose when your deduction is sound. Concealing what you have already uncovered would only waste our time."

}
 "However," Meridith continues, tapping one finger on the stone table, "your analysis contains two critical errors. First, we did not bury thousands of commercial stat boosters in the earth. We acquired those units to strip their components, constructing industrial-scale extraction arrays far more potent than hand-held relics. Second, I never stated you would be denied access to the Magium."

"You expect us to believe that?" I challenge. "You just confirmed the tournament is a fabricated harvest, yet you claim the prize remains real?"

"You misunderstand the offer," Meridith says, leaning forward over the table. "The eventual tournament winner receives nothing. That spectacle ends in ash. But I am prepared to grant you access to our private node. You, and every member of your party. In exchange for a specific service."

"What service?" Kate asks, her fingers tightening on the grip of the heirloom scepter.

"Consider the origin," Meridith says, her voice dropping into cold, deliberate clarity. "This entire tournament—the alliances, the siphons, the deception—exists for one solitary purpose. Ever since the destruction of Olmnar twenty years ago, the lessathi leadership and the ruling kings have operated under a unified pact. After two decades of preparation, the apparatus is complete. By the tournament's conclusion, justice will be executed. Eiden will die."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Ending
TEXT:

 "You want us to kill Eiden for you?" Hadrik asks, folding his arms over his chest.

"No," Meridith replies. "Our primary strike will finish him. That is the sole destination for the energy we harvest."

"Then what role do we serve?" Daren asks.

"Contingency," Meridith says. "If the past weeks proved anything, it is that meticulous planning can collapse under unforeseen fortune. I require insurance. This is no suicide errand. Should Eiden survive our opening barrage through some miracle, he will be shattered—his reserves drained, his wards fractured. Weakened to a degree your company can dismantle him with minimal risk. If you agree to act as that contingency, I will grant you unrestricted access to the Magium node we visited."

"Will you disclose your plan?" Daren asks.

"I will not," Meridith states plainly. "If Eiden captures your company and uncovers that secret, twenty years of preparation evaporates. I cannot take that risk."

 "You promise Magium access if we intervene," Kate says, "but what if your plan executes flawlessly? If your strike destroys Eiden without our involvement, what then?"

"The reward stands," Meridith says. "Serving as our contingency requires you to maintain your presence on Varathia until the tournament concludes. That means progressing through the brackets and fulfilling objectives so the monarchs have no pretext to deport you. Fulfill that obligation, and your prize is guaranteed regardless of whether your blades are drawn."

"And the kings know nothing of this pact?" Kate asks.

"Nothing," Meridith confirms. "The royal courts are unaware we possess a functional node. They believe access is strictly monopolized by the crown. This covenant is struck with me, as high leader of the lessathi, independent of tournament stewardship. Maintain your standing, stand ready if our primary strike falters, and all of you claim the prize you crossed the ocean to find, without needing to win the tournament."

 She rises from her seat, smoothing her dark robes with deliberate composure, her green gaze sweeping across each of us in turn.

"The terms are laid before you," Meridith says. "The choice remains yours."

Silence settles over the chamber as she pauses, letting the immense weight of the bargain fill the room.

"What is your answer?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10c-Credits, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10c-Credits)


ID: B3-Ch10c-Credits
TEXT:

 The eleventh chapter of the third book is now available, however it is not the final chapter of the book. This book will have 12 chapters in total.

I am very sorry for the huge delay. I've had a very bad stress-induced writer's block that started with the invasion of Ukraine (due to living in a neighboring country), and I haven't been able to regain the same writing speed since then.

I realize this isn't really an excuse, and I'm trying hard to regain a decent writing speed, but for the moment I had to split the remaining content until the end of book 3 into two separate chapters, due to the long distance between the checkpoints, so I decided to publish 'chapter 11' separately.

The final chapter was always meant to be split into two parts with a time skip between them, like chapter 10, so you can at least rest assured that the ending of chapter 11 won't leave you on a big cliffhanger.

Also, due to the many delays I've had in the past, I've decided to make the estimate much more vague this time, because I still don't know how fast I'll be able to write.

